  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tournons La Page Niger
Description of the action : &#x201c;Citizen Participation for an effective rebuilding of governance in Niger&#x201d;.Relevance of the action to the objectives and priorities of the call for proposalsThe &#x201c;Citizen Participation for an effective rebuilding of governance in Niger&#x201d; project is perfectly aligned with the specific objectives and priorities of the call for proposals, particularly in terms of strengthening democratic governance, citizen participation and social accountability. The actionresponds to Niger&apos;surgent and specific needs in a political context of transition marked by the rebuilding of institutions. It aims to actively involve citizens in local and national governance, through participatory and inclusive mechanisms, while strengthening the transparency and efficiency of public services. The project contributes to building a more inclusive system of governance, while addressing the specific constraints encountered in the Maradi region, where local populations are often marginalized and have little involvement in decision-making processes. The action is also part of a synergy with other development initiatives, notably projects to strengthen local governance, fight corruption and promote human rights. This helps to avoid redundancies while reinforcing efforts to ensure accountability and inclusiveness in ongoing public reforms.Target groups, final beneficiaries and stakeholdersTarget groups:The project mainly targets 25 local Civil Society Organization (CSO) structures including: 25 young community leaders, 25 committed women, as well as local authorities from the 16 communes of the Maradi region. These groups have been chosen because of their key role in promoting inclusive and participatory governance. Local CSOs, in particular, often lack the technical and institutional resources needed tofully play their counter-power role, which justifies their priority inclusion in the action.Final beneficiaries :Local populations,in particular ordinary citizens in the 16 communes of theMaradi region, will benefit indirectly from the project through improved quality of public services and greater participation in the management of public affairs. The action also aims to strengthen relations of trust between the governors and the governed, thereby increasing social and political stability in the region.Key stakeholders:Local authorities, supervisory bodies (ARCOP, Inspection G&#xe9;n&#xe9;rale des Services), and the local media are key stakeholders. Their commitment is essential to ensure the effective implementation of reforms and social accountability actions. Consultations with these actors revealed a strong willingness to collaborate with civil society and promote more transparent and participatory governance.Technical and management capabilities:Local CSOs, though dynamic, require capacity building in project management, budget monitoring, advocacy and capitalizing on best practices. The project will provide training, work tools and technical support to fill these gaps, while drawing on localand national skills to facilitate the implementation of activities.</narrative>
  </description>
